title The number that looked like a fault
prompt Same lap, second increment -- the seed cadence on lap 3370 (N mod 5 == 0): project, run the four gates, surface the force-push
think Lap 3370 closes the rota cycle, so this is the seed cadence lap. ITINERARY settles the conflict between the cadence grant and custody gate %1 for itself: the projection and its four gates are agent-doable, the force-push is Keaton's hand, and an autonomous lap surfaces that rather than reading the grant in its own favour. Precedent 20260823.142302.
think The projection returned SOW_OK copied=1644 scrubbed=354 withheld=4 against the 6,948 the card recorded one lap earlier -- a 76 percent shortfall. Four laps of this arc had just been rosters that stopped matching after a fold, so 1644 read exactly like an allowlist gone stale.
think Counted git ls-files per allow path before believing it. tools alone returns 3,069 and the paths sum past 6,000, so the manifest was fine. Only then did the concurrent standing roster explain it: the roster carries a sow guard, sow re-projects, and sow_project.sh clears seed/ and rebuilds it in place. I had measured a directory that was being written.
think That is REDS %172's shape returned -- running the roster manufactured the artifact the next run reported -- moved from a scan's output onto the public projection, which is where it costs most. The misread is the cheap half. sow_leak_scan.sh, sow_witness.rish and publish-seed.sh all gate on what stands in seed/, so a gate reading a half-built tree has examined a set nobody chose. IDENT_CLEAN over a partial projection is a true statement about the wrong corpus.
think The lock refuses rather than queues, because a queued run still hands its reader a number from a tree that changed underneath them. A directory rather than a file, since mkdir is atomic where test-then-create is not. Released on EXIT, INT and TERM, and a lock left by a killed run names its dead pid so the next run clears it rather than wedging forever.
think Asked git rather than guessing where the lock may live -- git check-ignore -v seed.projection.lock answers .gitignore:8:/*, so the root is allowlisted and the lock is already ignored.
think The full control takes 171 seconds because release-on-exit is only true if the script reaches its exit, and reaching it means one real projection. Rather than pay that on every roster pass or drop the case in silence, the standing guard runs the four refusals in about a second and names the split in its own prose -- and release-on-exit stays gated, by sow itself: a leaked lock makes the next projection exit 3 and turns sow red.
obs A number nobody can tell apart from a real fault is a false red waiting to be booked. This one took a card entry from the previous lap to refute; without it I would have gone looking for a broken allowlist that was never broken. The prevention is worth more than the correction, because the next reader may not have the previous lap's number.
obs Folding row %191 moved its link depth with its text this time -- ten targets rewritten from ../ to ../../ before the file was written, rather than after readme_reach refused them. Row %192 paid for that lesson two hours ago.
obs The four seed gates all hold, read alone. The force-push stays custody gate %1 and is surfaced rather than crossed.
loom witness=sow_lock control_cases_full=6 control_cases_fast=4 control_fail=0 full_wall_s=171 fast_wall_s=1
loom projection=sow_project copied=6961 scrubbed=1066 withheld=122 raced_reading_copied=1644
loom roster=standing_equipment guards=49 committed_tree_green=47 of 47
file tools/fixtures/sow_project.sh the lock -- refuse rather than queue, a directory rather than a file, released on EXIT INT TERM, a dead holder's lock cleared
file tools/fixtures/sow_lock_control.sh six behaviors on real directories, four refusals bitten, with a fast mode for the standing path
file tools/s/sow_lock_witness.rish the 48th standing guard, naming in its own prose which case it skips and which guard covers it
file construction/standing-equipment.kyri sow_lock seated 48th
file construction/REDS.md rows %193 and %194; rows %191 and %192 folded with their link depth intact
file construction/archive/REDS-the-guard-that-was-right-and-unheard-rows-191.md the folded row, ten links moved to ../../ before writing
file construction/ITINERARY.md the seed cadence entry re-read alone, 48 guards, the force-push still surfaced
think Staging the lock work turned exec_bit red where it had been green minutes earlier -- the guard reads the index, so the new control counted only once staged. That is the run-again-after-git-add discipline earning its keep. plain_shebang_ratchet=59 against a ceiling of 58.
think The scan's own prose said these fixtures fall on touch. That reaches birth as well: a fixture born at 100644 grows the population the ceiling exists to shrink, so it is born with the bit. Gave sow_lock_control.sh and sow_project.sh their exec bits, ratchet fell to 57, lowered the ceiling to 57, and moved exec_bit_control.sh's planted counts with it -- 58 against 57 rather than 59 against 58 -- since a control proving a ceiling the tree no longer holds proves nothing.
think Then docs-implementation-sync sent me to .claude/rules/exec-bit.md, and running document_mirror_scan.sh write left the Cursor twin still saying 58. The descriptor had never named it. Counting the pairs: 40 stand, 1 registered.
think Registering the other 39 would have been the wrong repair, and this is the finding. document-mirrors.brix proves homes BYTE-IDENTICAL; a .mdc twin is a transform -- frontmatter on top, links rewritten .md to .mdc, and a closing cross-pointer each file aims at the other on purpose. Register all forty and every one reds forever, which is a guard nobody keeps. REDS %187's shape with a sharper edge: the roster was not merely short, it was the wrong kind of roster, and a short roster invites the reflex of adding rows.
think Measured under a declared transform: 40 pairs, 4 agree, 36 drift, plus one Cursor rule with no Claude canonical at all. The drift runs BOTH ways -- reds-first's twin names work-in-progress/REDS.md, a path that moved to construction/, so it is behind; send-word's twin carries an ls-remote pre-push guard and a two-remote push discipline the Claude side has never held, so it is ahead. A bulk merge in either direction silently deletes a live safety rule, and send-word is the proof the deleted one could be a guard against pushing to a remote that refuses.
think So the guard ratchets rather than gates, and the reconciliation is surfaced as custody gate 7 rather than attempted. Built the transform tight as well as generous: the control proves one changed word bites, a paragraph on one side only bites, and a stale path bites, because a transform too forgiving to see those reports agreement over two files saying different things, which is worse than no meter.
obs A repair that follows the rule can still find the rule's own mechanism reaching one fortieth of its subject. The two-line ceiling edit was the smallest change in the round and it opened the widest finding.
obs The half that was mine to repair is repaired: the exec-bit pair leaves this lap in agreement rather than one worse. The other 36 stay a reading, and a reading is Keaton's.
loom witness=rule_twin pairs=40 agree=4 drift=36 ceiling=36 cursor_only=1 control_cases=12 control_fail=0
loom witness=exec_bit ratchet_before=59 ratchet_after=57 ceiling_before=58 ceiling_after=57
file tools/fixtures/exec_bit_scan.sh the ceiling falls 58 to 57, with the reason named at the constant
file tools/fixtures/exec_bit_control.sh planted counts moved with the ceiling, 58 against 57
file .claude/rules/exec-bit.md the ratchet row, the falls-on-touch-reaches-birth reading, and the control's new numbers
file .cursor/rules/exec-bit.mdc the twin brought back into agreement under the transform
file tools/fixtures/rule_twin_scan.sh the transform declared in four steps, drift under a ceiling that only falls
file tools/fixtures/rule_twin_control.sh twelve behaviors, the transform proven tight as well as generous
file tools/r/rule_twin_witness.rish the 49th standing guard, naming the reconciliation it will not attempt
file construction/ITINERARY.md custody gate 7 -- the 36-pair reconciliation, surfaced
recommend keep-going the seed cadence is met and its gate is surfaced, so the next crux is docs/CRYPTO.md at 69,099 bytes -- the last living document past the 24,576-byte bound a split would serve, and it wants a drawn guard rather than a reused one, since a docs-shelf page has no .rye directory beside it for module_roster_scan.sh to read. Beside it stands the reading this arc measured twice and left open: no guard reads a Ceiling: header anywhere in the tree, and the one page that declares one drifted past it without a sound.
